BODY = '''<p>A walker leaves little room to carry a cup of tea or a phone. Trays, pouches and baskets fill that gap, and the advice about them is short. This page puts together what Mayo Clinic and MedlinePlus say, the sizes and fit notes that Drive Medical publishes for its trays and basket, and what we could not find.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>These are general tips.</strong> A physical therapist can check how your walker is set up and what it is safe to carry.</p>

<h2>What Mayo Clinic and MedlinePlus Say</h2>
<p>Mayo Clinic's "Tips for choosing and using walkers" says options and accessories can make a walker easier to use, and gives three examples. Trays can help you carry food, drinks and other items. A pouch attached to the side of a walker can carry books, a phone or other items. Baskets are useful if you use your walker when you go shopping.</p>
<p>The same page adds two cautions. Whatever walker you choose, don't overload it, and keep it in good working order. Worn-out or loose rubber caps or grips can raise the risk of falling, and so can brakes that are too loose or too tight.</p>
<p>MedlinePlus, in "Using a walker" (reviewed April 2025), says to attach a small bag or basket to your walker to hold small items, so that you can keep both hands on your walker. It also says not to use stairs and escalators unless a physical therapist has trained you to use them with your walker, and that the walker should be adjusted to your height, with the handles at hip level and your elbows slightly bent. It does not mention trays.</p>

<h2>Tray, Pouch or Basket</h2>
<table class="art-table"><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>What the sources say it is for</th><th>Drive Medical's version</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tray</td><td>Food, drinks and other items (Mayo Clinic)</td><td>Two trays, 10124 and 10125, in the table below</td></tr><tr><td>Side pouch or bag</td><td>Books, a phone or small items, with both hands on the walker (Mayo Clinic, MedlinePlus)</td><td>Not covered by the Drive pages we read; its steel rollator comes with a pouch under the seat</td></tr><tr><td>Basket</td><td>Shopping, small items (Mayo Clinic, MedlinePlus)</td><td>Walker Basket 10200B, with a plastic insert tray and cup holder</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The choice comes down to what you carry. Mayo Clinic describes a tray as a place for food and drinks. A pouch or basket holds small items, which fits MedlinePlus's point about keeping both hands on the walker.</p>

<h2>Drive Medical's Trays and Basket</h2>
<table class="art-table"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Size</th><th>Cup holders</th><th>Fit, per Drive</th><th>Load limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10124 Universal Walker Tray</td><td>23 by 17 by 1.5 in</td><td>One</td><td>Most manufacturers' walkers</td><td>Not listed</td></tr><tr><td>10125 Walker Tray</td><td>16 by 12 by 2 in</td><td>Two</td><td>Most manufacturers' 2-button walkers</td><td>Not listed</td></tr><tr><td>10200B Walker Basket</td><td>Not listed</td><td>Insert tray with a cup holder</td><td>"All 1-inch folding walkers"</td><td>Not listed</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Both trays are durable plastic with a limited one-year warranty, and Drive says the smaller one folds down when you are not using it. The basket page gives no size, material or warranty. We read Drive's pages for these three, and could not read other brands' tray pages, so the table covers one maker.</p>

<h2>Check the Fit Before You Buy</h2>
<p>Drive says its tray fits "most" walkers and names 2-button walkers for the smaller one, without listing models. Drive's basket page says it is for use with all 1-inch folding walkers, without saying what the 1 inch measures. So the first step is to look at your own walker: whether it is a folding two-button walker, a rollator or something else, and how wide the frame is where a tray would rest. A tray that wobbles on your frame is the wrong tray.</p>
<p>A tray should not change how you stand at the walker. MedlinePlus says the handles should be at hip level with your elbows slightly bent, so check that you can still hold the grips that way with the tray fitted. Our <a href="/tools/cane-and-walker-height-checker/">cane and walker height checker</a> compares your wrist height with the walkers in our guides.</p>

<h2>The Load Limit Question</h2>
<p>Mayo Clinic says not to overload a walker, but it gives no number. Drive lists no load limit on either tray page, and we found no standard for tray load limits. Other brands may publish one, so read the listing for the tray you pick and treat a figure you cannot find as a reason to carry less. A phone, a book, drinks and small items are the kind of load the sources describe. Anything heavy is not, so ask a physical therapist what is safe for you.</p>

<h2>Using One Safely</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep both hands on the walker.</strong> MedlinePlus suggests a small bag or basket for exactly that reason.</li>
<li><strong>Do not overload it.</strong> Mayo Clinic's wording is "don't overload."</li>
<li><strong>Check the walker itself.</strong> Mayo Clinic links worn rubber caps and grips, and loose or tight brakes, to a higher risk of falling.</li>
<li><strong>Skip stairs and escalators</strong> unless a physical therapist has trained you to use them with your walker (MedlinePlus).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Walker Tray Questions</h2>
<h3>Do trays fit rollators?</h3>
<p>Drive's tray pages do not mention rollators. Its tray 10125 is listed for 2-button walkers. Rollators usually have storage built in. In our <a href="/best-rollators/">rollator guide</a>, the Drive steel rollator comes with a pouch under the seat, per Drive's page, and the Medline Steel Rollator has a bag under the seat, per its listing.</p>
<h3>Is a tray or a basket better?</h3>
<p>It depends on what you carry. Mayo Clinic describes a tray for food and drinks and a basket for shopping. MedlinePlus recommends a small bag or basket so both hands stay on the walker, and it does not mention a tray.</p>
<h3>How much can a walker tray hold?</h3>
<p>Drive does not say for its two trays. Mayo Clinic's advice is not to overload the walker.</p>
<h3>Where can I read more?</h3>
<p>Mayo Clinic's "Tips for choosing and using walkers" and MedlinePlus's "Using a walker" are linked below. For choosing the walker itself, see <a href="/articles/cane-walker-or-rollator/">cane, walker or rollator</a>.</p>'''

